  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1259 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nA weak frontal boundary is forecast to sink south towards our region \ntoday, making passage tonight. Models generally depict higher dew \npoints pooling ahead of the boundary so despite warmer high \ntemperatures in the low to mid 80s (even upper 80s in our \nnorthwest), minimum RH's are expected to be near or slightly higher \nthan yesterday (mainly 30-35%). Areas near the KY/TN border have the \nbest potential to see lower values. Winds will be out of the \nsouthwest today and will be strongest in our north, where deeper \nmixing may lead to some gustiness this afternoon. \n\nBehind the frontal passage winds become northeasterly on Thursday. \nDespite this, actual high temperatures will remain similar in most \nplaces. Drier air will mix down the column in the afternoon with \nsome guidance suggesting dew points falling into the upper 30s to \nmid 40s across much of the region. Confidence is highest this will \noccur in our north so for now have kept these lower values up there \nwhich results in RH's falling into the 20-25% range. Something to \nmonitor though as potential is there for a good chunk of our cwa to \nwitness RH's of 25% or lower on Thursday.\n\nTropical Depression Nine has formed in the southwestern Gulf and \nconfidence is increasing that this will strengthen and become the \nAtlantic Basin's first Hurricane of the 2026 season. This system is \nforecast to make landfall somewhere in the vicinity of New Orleans \nto Mobile Friday night/early Saturday morning. The rain shield \nassociated with it should quickly spread north across the TN Valley \nduring the day on Saturday, potentially reaching southern portions \nof our cwa by midday or afternoon. Timing is still somewhat \nuncertain, but latest trends have been a bit quicker. So the daytime \nhours on Saturday may not end up being completely dry anymore, at \nleast for the southern half of our area. As pwats increase to 2\", \nheavier swaths of rain are anticipated Saturday evening into the \nnight, possibly continuing into the day on Sunday. However, if the \nfaster solution pans out, the system may be departing leaving behind \nmainly dry conditions by Sunday afternoon. We should have a better \nhandle on timing and exact track within the next 48 hours or so.\n\nFor now, best to prepare for an unsettled weekend ahead, with \nmoderate to heavy rain impacting the region leading to rainfall \ntotals potentially in the 1-2\" range. Can't rule out some localized \nhigher amounts too that may lead to some flooding potential. If the \ncenter of the weakening tropical system tracks over our cwa, there \ncould also be an increased concern for some severe thunderstorms \nincluding a few tornadoes. Right now that threat appears highest to \nour east, but that could easily shift westward. We also may have \nsome strong gusty gradient winds to deal with. Currently the GFS is \nshowing potential at 30-40 mph gusts.\n\nOnce the tropical system departs, upper ridging builds back into the \nregion allowing temperatures to return to above normal readings in \nthe 80s early next week.",
